WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.093 --> 00:00:08.498
Miss Martha Meacham kept the little bakery on the corner (the one where you go up three steps, and the bell tinkles when you open the door).

2
00:00:08.499 --> 00:00:17.558
Miss Martha was forty, her bankbook showed a credit of two thousand dollars, and she possessed two false teeth and a sympathetic heart.

3
00:00:17.559 --> 00:00:23.328
Many people have married whose chances to do so were much inferior to Miss Martha's.

4
00:00:23.329 --> 00:00:28.864
Two or three times a week, a customer came in whom she began to take an interest.

5
00:00:28.865 --> 00:00:35.013
He was a middle-aged man, wearing spectacles and a brown beard trimmed to a careful point.

6
00:00:35.014 --> 00:00:38.625
He spoke English with a strong German accent.

7
00:00:38.626 --> 00:00:44.283
His clothes were worn and darned in places, and wrinkled and baggy in others.

8
00:00:44.284 --> 00:00:48.077
But he looked neat and had very good manners.

9
00:00:48.078 --> 00:00:54.286
He always bought two loaves of stale bread. Fresh bread was five cents a loaf.

10
00:00:54.287 --> 00:01:00.361
Stale ones were two for five. Never did he call for anything but stale bread.

11
00:01:00.362 --> 00:01:08.477
Once, Miss Martha saw a red and brown stain on his fingers. She was sure then that he was an artist and very poor.

12
00:01:08.478 --> 00:01:16.391
No doubt he lived in a garret, where he painted pictures and ate stale bread and thought of the good things to eat in Miss Martha's bakery.

13
00:01:16.392 --> 00:01:29.936
Often when Miss Martha sat down to her chops and light rolls and jam and tea, she would sigh and wish that the gentle-mannered artist might share her tasty meal instead of eating his dry crust in that drafty attic.

14
00:01:29.937 --> 00:01:35.308
Miss Martha's heart, as you have been told, was a sympathetic one.

15
00:01:35.309 --> 00:01:46.026
In order to test her theory as to his occupation, she brought from her room one day a painting that she had bought at a sale, and set it against the shelves behind the bread counter.

16
00:01:46.027 --> 00:01:55.594
It was a Venetian scene. A splendid marble palazzio (so it said on the picture) stood in the foreground─or rather forewater.

17
00:01:55.595 --> 00:02:05.299
For the rest, there were gondolas (with the lady trailing her hand in the water), clouds, sky, and beautiful play of light and shadow in plenty.

18
00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:08.117
No artist could fail to notice it.

19
00:02:08.118 --> 00:02:11.080
Two days afterward, the customer came in.

20
00:02:11.081 --> 00:02:20.667
"Two loaves of stale bread, if you please. You have here a fine picture, madame," he said while she was wrapping up the bread.

21
00:02:20.668 --> 00:02:25.389
"Yes?" says Miss Martha, reveling in her own cunning.

22
00:02:25.390 --> 00:02:33.994
"I do so admire art and"—no, it would not do to say "artists" so early—"and paintings," she substituted.

23
00:02:33.995 --> 00:02:36.581
"You think it is a good picture?"

24
00:02:36.582 --> 00:02:45.893
"The balance," said the customer, "is not in good drawing. The perspective of it is not true. Good morning, madame."

25
00:02:45.894 --> 00:02:49.020
He took his bread, bowed, and hurried out.

26
00:02:49.021 --> 00:02:54.387
Yes, he must be an artist. Miss Martha took the picture back to her room.

27
00:02:54.388 --> 00:03:01.011
How gently and kindly his eyes shone behind his spectacles! What a broad brow he had!

28
00:03:01.012 --> 00:03:10.383
To be able to judge perspective at a glance─and to live on stale bread! But genius often has to struggle before it is recognized.

29
00:03:10.384 --> 00:03:22.705
What a thing it would be for art and perspective if genius were backed by two thousand dollars in the bank, a bakery, and a sympathetic heart to─But these were daydreams, Miss Martha.

30
00:03:22.706 --> 00:03:31.125
Often now when he came he would chat for a while across the showcase. He seemed to crave Miss Martha's cheerful words.

31
00:03:31.126 --> 00:03:38.915
He kept on buying stale bread─never a cake, never a pie, never one of her delicious Sally Lunns.

32
00:03:38.916 --> 00:03:42.764
She thought he began to look thinner and discouraged.

33
00:03:42.765 --> 00:03:49.615
Her heart ached to add something good to eat to his meager purchase, but her courage failed at the act.

34
00:03:49.616 --> 00:03:54.818
She did not dare affront him. She knew the pride of artists.

35
00:03:54.819 --> 00:03:59.937
Miss Martha took to wearing her blue-dotted silk blouse behind the counter. In

36
00:03:59.938 --> 00:04:08.742
the back room, she cooked a mysterious compound of quince seeds and borax. Ever so many people use it for the complexion. One

37
00:04:08.743 --> 00:04:16.085
day, the customer came in as usual, laid his nickel on the showcase, and called for his stale loaves. While

38
00:04:16.086 --> 00:04:23.499
Miss Martha was reaching for them, there was a great tooting and clanging, and a fire engine came lumbering past. The

39
00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:31.794
customer hurried to the door to look, as anyone will. Suddenly inspired, Miss Martha seized the opportunity. On

40
00:04:31.795 --> 00:04:38.811
the bottom shelf behind the counter was a pound of fresh butter that the dairyman had left ten minutes before. With

41
00:04:38.812 --> 00:04:48.331
a bread knife, Miss Martha made a deep slash in each of the stale loaves, inserted a generous quantity of butter, and pressed the loaves tight again. When

42
00:04:48.332 --> 00:04:53.589
the customer turned once more, she was tying the paper around them. When

43
00:04:53.590 --> 00:05:03.052
he had gone, after an unusually pleasant little chat, Miss Martha smiled to herself, but not without a slight fluttering of the heart. Had

44
00:05:03.053 --> 00:05:10.170
she been too bold? Would he take offense? But surely not. There was no language of edibles. Butter

45
00:05:10.171 --> 00:05:13.842
was no emblem of unmaidenly forwardness. For

46
00:05:13.843 --> 00:05:22.023
a long time that day, her mind dwelt on the subject. She imagined the scene when he should discover her little deception. He

47
00:05:22.024 --> 00:05:25.338
would lay down his brushes and palette. There

48
00:05:25.339 --> 00:05:31.828
would stand his easel with the picture he was painting in which the perspective was beyond criticism. He

49
00:05:31.829 --> 00:05:38.761
would prepare for his luncheon of dry bread and water. He would slice into a loaf─ah! Miss

50
00:05:38.762 --> 00:05:45.425
Martha blushed. Would he think of the hand that placed it there as he ate? Would he─ The

51
00:05:45.426 --> 00:05:52.446
front door bell jangled viciously. Somebody was coming in, making a great deal of noise. Miss

52
00:05:52.447 --> 00:05:57.168
Martha hurried to the front. Two men were there. One

53
00:05:57.169 --> 00:06:04.284
was a young man smoking a pipe─a man she had never seen before. The other was her artist. His

54
00:06:04.285 --> 00:06:11.332
face was very red, his hat was on the back of his head, his hair was wildly rumpled. He

55
00:06:11.334 --> 00:06:15.660
clenched his two fists and shook them ferociously at Miss Martha. "You

56
00:06:15.667 --> 00:06:24.291
stupid woman!" he shouted with extreme loudness; and then "Tausendonfer!" or something like it in German. The

57
00:06:24.292 --> 00:06:27.287
young man tried to draw him away. "I

58
00:06:27.288 --> 00:06:32.989
will not go," he said angrily, "before I have told her." He

59
00:06:32.990 --> 00:06:36.221
made a bass drum of Miss Martha's counter. "You

60
00:06:36.222 --> 00:06:46.770
have ruined me," he cried, his blue eyes blazing behind his spectacles. "I will tell you. You are a meddlesome old cat! "

61
00:06:46.771 --> 00:06:56.471
Miss Martha leaned weakly against the shelves and laid one hand on her blue-dotted silk blouse. The young man took the other by the collar.

62
00:06:56.472 --> 00:07:05.018
"Come on," he said, "you've said enough." He dragged the angry one out the door to the sidewalk, and then came back.

63
00:07:05.019 --> 00:07:10.007
"Guess you ought to be told, ma'am," he said, "what the row is about.

64
00:07:10.008 --> 00:07:15.967
That's Blumberger. He's an architectural draftsman. I work in the same office with him.

65
00:07:15.968 --> 00:07:23.000
"He's been working hard for three months drawing a plan for a new city hall. It was a prize competition.

66
00:07:23.001 --> 00:07:30.293
He finished inking the lines yesterday. You know, a draftsman always makes his drawing in pencil first.

67
00:07:30.294 --> 00:07:38.062
When it's done, he rubs out the pencil lines with handfuls of stale bread crumbs. That's better than India rubber.

68
00:07:38.063 --> 00:07:40.998
"Blumberger's been buying the bread here.

69
00:07:40.999 --> 00:07:52.131
Well, today─well, you know, ma'am, that butter isn't─well, Blumberger's plan isn't good for anything now except to wrap up sandwiches."

70
00:07:52.132 --> 00:07:55.208
Miss Martha went into the back room.

71
00:07:55.209 --> 00:08:02.141
She took off the blue-dotted silk blouse and put on the old brown wool shirt she used to wear.

72
00:08:02.142 --> 00:08:08.209
Then she poured the quince seed and borax mixture out of the window into the ash can.

